# Members of wheel may use sudo without re-entering a password.
#
# This device is administered over SSH by the single account the setup wizard
# creates, and that account's key is the only credential the :8080 SSH page can
# display or revoke. A sudo password on top of it is not a second factor - it
# is a second secret guarding the same authority, one the device has no way to
# show, rotate or revoke, and one that has to be typed by hand on a headless
# appliance nobody has a console for.
#
# The trade is deliberate and worth stating plainly: whoever holds that
# account's SSH key holds root. That is the property we want, because revoking
# the key from the SSH page now revokes root along with it - there is exactly
# one thing to take away, instead of a second root credential living outside
# everything that manages this device.
#
# It does move the weakest link. With this rule in place, SSH password
# authentication is the shortest path to root, so turn it off from the SSH page
# once a key is installed; that toggle is guarded against locking you out.
#
# The shipped default account (user / 147147, postmarketOS's published
# default) is in wheel too, until setup replaces it. Its password is public, so
# while it is live sshd refuses it everywhere except over the USB cable
# (biscuit-firstboot), and refuses every login from the open setup network
# (/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/40-biscuit.conf).
%wheel ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL
